# Local model resolver (P4 Task 3): machine-local qualification state and bridge log, never
# committed. loop-bridge-log.jsonl already defaults outside the repo
# (~/.claude/loop-bridge-log.jsonl, ollama_bridge.py fix round 1 I4); this entry is belt and
# braces for a --log override that lands inside the repo, not the primary control.
.claude/local-model-state.json
.claude/local-model-config.json
.claude/.maintenance.lock
loop-bridge-log.jsonl

# aider-setup: the operator's own live aider config, used only to run this
# machine's tests against the real driver. It carries this machine's absolute
# home path (build_kit.py's scrub() strips that same path when it copies a
# *different* template into the shipped kit - this file is not that template,
# it is the fixture the tests run against). Regenerate it from
# .claude/skills/aider-setup/config/aider.conf.yml, substituting your own home.
.claude/skills/aider-setup/scripts/build/aider.conf.yml.saved

# Self-improvement loop: machine-local state, never repository content.
# .rt-green.json is the proof that the offline suite passed, with a hash per code file;
# .rt-undo/ holds the previous version of any file the loop changed, pruned to 20.
.rt-green.json
.rt-undo/
# .rt-mirrors.json is install.ps1 -Manifest's record of the verdict it computed for
# every mirror, plus the policy hash it used. Machine-local, like the green stamp.
.rt-mirrors.json
.claude/local-capability-probe.json


# Machine-local configuration generated from a versioned template. A file a script generates
# is never a tracked file: this one carries THIS machine's vault path and Obsidian executable,
# and the real one lives in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, which setup.ps1 writes additively.
#   CLAUDE.md  <- CLAUDE.template.md, destination ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md
# ROOT-ANCHORED with a leading slash: a bare "CLAUDE.md" would match at any depth and would
# ignore .claude/CLAUDE.md, which is hand-written and must stay tracked.
/CLAUDE.md

# .claude/settings.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It is the SHARED project scope and it has
# to reach GitHub: a routine running on the web clones this repository and has no ~/.claude of
# ours, so a committed project file is the only way it learns which plugin marketplaces to
# trust when it reviews a pull request. It was ignored for a while because setup.ps1 used to
# GENERATE it - 8324 bytes carrying twelve absolute paths through one user profile. THAT file
# was unpublishable. The one here is hand-written: marketplace and plugin declarations only,
# no path, no account name, no secret.
#
# What must never go into it, because a cloud runner is not this machine:
#   - hooks. The global file already declares them; a project copy makes every hook run TWICE,
#     which on 2026-08-30 pushed session-hooks-inventory.py past its timeout and made a healthy
#     session report no inventory at all. Their commands are also C:\Users\... paths that do
#     not exist on a Linux runner.
#   - env, permissions or statusLine carrying an absolute path.
#   - any token: GITHUB_TOKEN and friends stay in the environment.
# The machine-specific counterpart is settings.local.json, which is personal and stays ignored.
/.claude/settings.local.json


# graphify: derived from the files, regenerable and disposable. One graph per repo,
# built from the repo root; a graph under a subdirectory is a partial graph (see
# local-writer.md, "Updating the graph").
graphify-out/

# Obsidian vault state under the repo (agent memory: user-specific, evolves with
# usage, never versioned). See PR #13 / issue #14 NEW-2.
.obsidian/

# Local git worktrees (superpowers:using-git-worktrees), isolated checkouts for unit branches.
/.worktrees/

# form service: local environment, never committed
deploy/.env

# parse cache: derived from the source PDF, never committed
*.parsed.md
*.parsed.meta.json
